// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-09-12 (Saturday): Xenharmonic tuning experiment — 15edo
// ============================================================================
//
// THE TUNING: 15edo divides the octave into 15 equal steps of 1200/15 = 80c
// each. Real historical precedent, not a novelty: Easley Blackwood's "Twelve
// Microtonal Etudes for Electronic Music" (1980) includes an etude written in
// 15-tone equal temperament, and the system is still called "Blackwood
// temperament" after him. Checked against all three prior Saturdays before
// writing a note of this (17edo, 19edo, 31edo) — see ARRANGEMENT below for
// what's actually fresh this week, not last cycle's template with new
// numbers.
//
// WHY 15edo, SPECIFICALLY (a different character than 17/19/31's "which
// third is sweeter" story): 15 = 3 x 5, so the octave splits into three
// EXACTLY EQUAL blocks of 5 steps each — 5 steps = 400c, which is also
// 12-TET's major third exactly (coincidence of the two systems' math, not an
// approximation). That means the interval from any note up 5 steps is
// acoustically identical in structure everywhere in the scale — transposing
// a phrase by +5 steps doesn't just "sound similar," it reproduces the exact
// same scale-step relationships, because 5+5+5=15=octave. This is the
// "Blackwood illusion": a modulation that isn't really a modulation. (The
// tradeoff: with the octave locked to 3 equal thirds, the fifth can't land
// justly — 9 steps = 720c, sharp of pure 3:2 (701.96c) by 18c, complemented
// by a correspondingly flat fourth at 6 steps = 480c. Bright/tense 5ths,
// perfectly self-similar 3rds — that tension IS the tuning's character.)
//
// ARRANGEMENT: per CREATIVE-DIRECTION.md's 10 Sep genre-first correction,
// structure (rhythm/arrangement/sidechain) is genre-driven project-wide now,
// not raga-performance-form-driven — so unlike a raga alapana (which
// MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md correctly argues has no metric pulse), a xenharmonic
// piece framed as a dance-genre track legitimately carries one. Built as a
// lean four-on-the-floor techno pulse (kick + off-beat hats only, real
// duckorbit sidechain into the sub per MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §10's genre-correct
// techno values — hard/fast, not a decorative extra layer) carrying a CANON
// on top: the lead phrase plays twice, once straight and once transposed
// +5 steps and nudged a quarter-cycle late, in a contrasting timbre panned
// to the opposite side (Pink Floyd stereo-duel device, §7). Because +5 is
// exactly the self-similar interval above, the second voice isn't a
// "different" melody chasing the first — it's the identical shape appearing
// to modulate while actually just restating itself, which is the whole
// point of this tuning made audible rather than described. Neither the
// separate-pad drone (17edo), the Sound-on-Sound feedback drone (19edo), nor
// the sustained stacked-chord voicing (31edo) fit this week's point, so none
// are reused; synth basics throughout per MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5 (sawtooth/
// triangle/sine, no gm_* samples).
//
// MELODY: structural anchors are the tonic (step 0) and the sharp fifth
// (step 9), same role vadi/samvadi play on a raga day. Four alternating
// phrases over the 16-cycle clip:
//   A (call): 0 ~ 5 9 ~ 14 15 — two clean third-leaps (+5) plus a half-step
//             (14->15) leading-tone push up to the octave.
//   B (response): 15 9 ~ 5 ~ 0 — falls back down (fourth, then two thirds),
//             resolves on the tonic.
//   C (retrograde of A — the required QC-loop device, literal reversal):
//             15 14 ~ 9 5 ~ 0
//   D (peak, tension->release): A's own shape transposed +5 (the
//             self-similar interval) — 5 ~ 10 14 ~ 19 20 — then a tail
//             falling back through 14 9 5, deliberately NOT resolving to
//             tonic; the release only lands when the loop wraps to A's
//             opening 0.
// The canon voice runs this same <A B C D> a further +5 up and a quarter
// cycle late, so by the time both voices are sounding, the piece is quietly
// demonstrating 0 -> 5 -> 10 -> (15=octave) as one continuous, self-similar
// staircase.
//
// CYCLES: 16. setcpm(48) -> 16 * 60/48 = 20s.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(48)

let LEAD = "<[0 ~ 5 9 ~ 14 15] [15 9 ~ 5 ~ 0] [15 14 ~ 9 5 ~ 0] [5 ~ 10 14 ~ 19 20 ~ 14 9 5]>"

// call voice: straight, panned left, one slow filter swell across the whole
// clip (one arc, not several independently-wandering LFOs — MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE
// §10's "fewer layers/params" lesson applies to automation too)
$leadA: i(LEAD).xen("15edo")
  .sound("sawtooth")
  .attack(.01).decay(.15).sustain(.12).release(.18)
  .lpf(sine.range(600, 2400).slow(8))
  .lpq(6)
  .pan(.25)
  .gain(.34)
  .room(1.4)

// canon voice: same phrase, +5 steps (the self-similar interval) and a
// quarter-cycle late, contrasting timbre so the ear can track both lines
// independently (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE §2's contrast-for-legibility), panned right
$leadB: i(LEAD).add(5).xen("15edo")
  .late(.25)
  .sound("triangle")
  .attack(.02).decay(.2).sustain(.1).release(.22)
  .lpf(1500)
  .pan(.75)
  .gain(.24)
  .room(1.8)

// sub: parked on the tonic almost the whole time, one visit to the (sharp)
// fifth per 4 cycles — Goa trance bass convention already established in
// this project, genuinely fits a lean techno pulse too
$sub: i("<-15 -15 -15 -6>/4").xen("15edo")
  .sound("sine")
  .lpf(500)
  .attack(.02).release(.4)
  .gain(.32)
  .orbit(2)

// kick: the audible pulse AND the sidechain source in one line — real
// duckorbit sidechain (not the earlier `.duck(0)` no-op mistake), techno
// values per MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE §10 (hard, fast pump — the low end breathes
// with the kick)
$: s("bd*4")
  .duckorbit(2).duckattack(.01).duckdepth(.85)
  .gain(.85)
  .room(.12)

// hats: off-beat, minimal, unducked, out of the way — the one other element
// four-on-the-floor techno actually calls for, nothing beyond it
$: s("~ hh ~ hh ~ hh ~ hh").gain(.09).room(.18)
